+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — rhai
+
+Dependency added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version` (hegeltest 0.28.2
+requires rustc 1.86; rhai pins `rust-version = "1.66.0"`, so the dev-dependency MSRV
+would need maintainer sign-off if upstreamed). Shared generators live in
+`tests/common/mod.rs` (arithmetic expression trees with a checked-arithmetic oracle,
+a well-formed typed script generator, and a rhai-keyword token-soup generator modeled
+on `fuzz/fuzz_targets/`). All other tests were added to existing test files.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`tests/math.rs::test_int_arithmetic_matches_checked_reference`** — Generated
+   integer arithmetic expression trees (`+ - * / %`, unary `-`, full-range INT with
+   boosted boundaries) evaluated by rhai equal a Rust-side reference using
+   `checked_*` arithmetic; reference `None` must correspond to `ErrorArithmetic`.
+   *Evidence:* `src/packages/arithmetic.rs` and `src/func/builtin.rs` implement all
+   INT operators with `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul`/`checked_div`/
+   `checked_rem`/`checked_neg` under `#[cfg(not(feature = "unchecked"))]`; existing
+   `test_math` pins individual overflow cases. Literals are bound via `let` so all
+   arithmetic goes through the runtime operators (see "Unsound properties abandoned").
+
+2. **`tests/optimizer.rs::test_optimization_levels_agree`** — The same generated
+   well-formed script (typed let-bindings, if/else statements and expressions,
+   bounded `for` loops, assignments, final INT expression) produces the same value,
+   or the same error kind, under `OptimizationLevel::None`, `Simple` and `Full`.
+   The configuration is generated too: `set_fast_operators` is drawn per case, and
+   all three optimization levels are compared pairwise. *Evidence:* the optimizer is
+   semantics-preserving by design (`src/optimizer.rs`); existing `test_optimizer_run`
+   runs fixed scripts at all three levels; the fuzz targets generate
+   `(script, OptimizationLevel)` pairs.
+
+3. **`tests/tokens.rs::test_compile_never_panics_on_arbitrary_input`** — `compile`
+   never panics on arbitrary Unicode text or rhai-token soup. *Evidence:* `compile`
+   returns `ParseResult`; `fuzz/fuzz_targets/scripting.rs` exists for exactly this.
+
+4. **`tests/tokens.rs::test_compact_script_preserves_semantics`** — For generated
+   well-formed scripts, `compact_script` succeeds and the compacted script evaluates
+   to the same value/error kind as the original. *Evidence:* doc-comment on
+   `compact_script` (src/api/formatting.rs): "The output script is semantically
+   identical to the input script"; commit fcb384fd recently fixed exactly this
+   property (`< -` fused into `<-`).
+
+5. **`tests/tokens.rs::test_compact_script_output_recompiles`** — On token soup with
+   randomized whitespace: if the input compiles, `compact_script` succeeds and its
+   output compiles again. Targets the operator-adjacency bug class of fcb384fd.
+
+6. **`tests/operations.rs::test_max_operations_terminates_infinite_loop`** — Any
+   drawn positive `max_operations` limit (1..=10000) stops `loop { x += 1 }` with
+   `ErrorTooManyOperations`, without panic or hang. *Evidence:* `set_max_operations`
+   docs and existing `test_max_operations`.
+
+7. **`tests/operations.rs::test_eval_arbitrary_input_with_limits_never_panics`** —
+   With the full limit configuration used by rhai's own fuzz harness (string/array/
+   map/variables/functions/modules/call-levels/expr-depths/operations) and a drawn
+   optimization level, compiling+evaluating arbitrary text, token soup, or generated
+   scripts never panics, and the operations limit guarantees termination.
+
+8. **`tests/stack.rs::test_expr_depth_limit_never_panics`** — With expression-depth
+   limits drawn up to the build's default limits, compiling nested expressions of
+   drawn depth 0..=400 in four shapes (`(((1)))`, `1 + 1 + ...`, `- - - 1`,
+   `!(!(...))`) either compiles or fails with exactly `ParseErrorType::ExprTooDeep`,
+   never panicking. *Evidence:* `set_max_expr_depths` exists to bound parser
+   recursion; `test_stack_overflow_parsing` pins instances. (Originally drew limits
+   up to 200 — see "Suspected bugs" finding 2 and the ignored reproducer
+   `test_expr_depth_reproducer_stack_overflow`.)
+
+9. **`tests/string.rs::test_string_literal_escape_roundtrip`** — For arbitrary
+   Unicode strings, a rhai string literal built by per-character choice of verbatim
+   inclusion or `\U????????` escaping evaluates back to exactly the original string.
+   *Evidence:* `parse_string_literal` in src/tokenizer.rs implements the escape
+   decoder; existing `test_string` pins examples.
+
+10. **`tests/number_literals.rs::test_int_literal_display_roundtrip`** — Every INT
+    (full range plus boosted MIN/MAX) printed in decimal parses back as a rhai
+    literal to the same value. *Evidence:* tokenizer parses decimal literals with
+    `INT::from_str` and folds a leading `-` in unary position into the literal.
+
+11. **`tests/float.rs::test_float_literal_debug_roundtrip`** — Every finite FLOAT
+    printed with Rust's shortest-roundtrip `{:?}` parses back bit-identically
+    (compared via `to_bits`, so `-0.0` is distinguished). *Evidence:* tokenizer
+    parses float literals (including scientific notation) via `FloatWrapper::from_str`.
+
+12. **`tests/var_scope.rs::test_scope_push_then_eval_roundtrip`** — A value (INT,
+    bool, or String, drawn) pushed into a `Scope` under an arbitrary generated
+    identifier is returned unchanged by evaluating that identifier, and remains in
+    the scope afterwards. *Evidence:* `Scope::push`/`eval_with_scope` docs,
+    `test_var_scope`, and the fuzz harness pushing arbitrary named variables.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full suite (`cargo test`, default features, debug build): **all 61 test targets
+  pass**; 12 hegel properties pass at the default 100 cases; 1 test is `#[ignore]`d
+  by design (process-aborting stack-overflow reproducer, see below). No new warnings
+  are introduced by the added code.
+- **Liveness check:** temporarily inverted the string-roundtrip assertion; hegel
+  reported and shrank to the minimal counterexample (`s = ""`). Reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10x run:** all properties were run once at `test_cases = 1000`, then
+  reverted to the default. This pass is what exposed the stack-overflow finding in
+  property 8 (the default 100 cases missed it; it reproduced roughly once per 2-8
+  runs of 1000 cases). After constraining the limits generator (see below), 5
+  consecutive 1000-case runs of property 8 were clean.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+No test is left failing: both findings below were investigated and judged to be
+deliberate/out-of-contract behavior rather than library bugs. They are documented
+here because both are surprising and worth a maintainer's attention.
+
+1. **Parse-time unary-minus overflow silently converts INT to FLOAT.**
+   - *Minimal counterexample:* `-(-9223372036854775808)` evaluates to the *float*
+     `9223372036854775808.0`, while the semantically identical
+     `let x = -9223372036854775808; -x` raises `ErrorArithmetic` ("Negation
+     overflow").
+   - *Code path:* `src/parser.rs` lines 1876-1892 (`parse_unary`, "Negative integer"
+     arm): `num.checked_neg()` on an integer constant falls back on overflow to
+     `Expr::FloatConstant((-(num as FLOAT)).into(), pos)` at parse time. Runtime
+     negation (`src/packages/arithmetic.rs` line 160) errors instead.
+   - *Why not filed as a bug:* the float fallback is explicit, deliberate code (with
+     a `no_float` variant returning `MalformedNumber`), so it is designed behavior —
+     but it makes negation semantics differ between constant and non-constant
+     operands of the same value, which maintainers may want to document.
+
+2. **Raising `set_max_expr_depths` beyond the defaults can crash the process by
+   physical stack overflow (debug builds, 2 MiB thread).**
+   - *Minimal reproducer:* `engine.set_max_expr_depths(187, 187)` then
+     `engine.compile("1 + 1 + ... + 1")` (185 terms) on a standard 2 MiB thread in a
+     debug build → `fatal runtime error: stack overflow, aborting` (SIGABRT, kills
+     the whole test process). Kept as the `#[ignore]`d test
+     `tests/stack.rs::test_expr_depth_reproducer_stack_overflow` per the skill's
+     process-abort rule.
+   - *Code path:* recursive descent in `src/parser.rs` (`parse_expr`/`parse_binary_op`
+     /`parse_unary`); the depth limit check only errors once depth *exceeds* the
+     configured limit, so a compile that stays under a large limit recurses that
+     deep with large debug-build frames.
+   - *Why not filed as a bug:* `set_max_expr_depths` documents no stack-safety
+     promise (0 means *unlimited*); the defaults are deliberately build-dependent
+     (32/16 debug vs 64/32 release, `src/api/limits.rs`), and rhai's own fuzz
+     harnesses choose `(50, 5)` — all evidence that matching the limit to the
+     available stack is the caller's responsibility. Still, users who raise the
+     limit modestly (e.g. 187) on default threads get a process abort, not an error.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Unary minus on any constant expression follows runtime checked semantics"** —
+  first version of property 1 rendered literals inline, so `Neg(Lit(INT::MIN))`
+  rendered as `-(-9223372036854775808)` and hit the parse-time float fallback
+  (finding 1 above) instead of the runtime `checked_neg` error. The property was
+  asserting runtime semantics for something rhai deliberately resolves at parse
+  time. Reworked (not weakened): literals are now bound to `let` variables so every
+  operation goes through the runtime operators across the full INT domain.
+
+- **"Any configured expression-depth limit is physically stack-safe"** — first
+  version of property 8 drew limits up to 200 and aborted the process (finding 2
+  above). The library never promises that; the generator was over-broad on the
+  *configuration* axis. The committed property draws limits up to the build's own
+  defaults (the range the library itself vouches for by shipping), and the original
+  crash is preserved as an ignored reproducer plus this report entry.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and the Rust reference worked well; the workflow (fuzz targets as
+evidence, generate-the-configuration, liveness check, 10x exploratory run) directly
+produced the two findings above. Specific notes:
+
+- **The 10x exploratory run pulled its weight.** The stack-overflow finding was
+  invisible at 100 cases and appeared only at 1000 (a boundary conjunction:
+  limit drawn high *and* nesting drawn just below the limit). Exactly as SKILL.md
+  predicts.
+- **Gap: edition-2015/2018 `panic!`/`assert!` message semantics.** The reference
+  covers edition 2015 (`extern crate hegel`) but not that in edition ≤2018 crates,
+  `panic!("... {x}")`/`assert!(cond, "... {x}")` with a single string argument does
+  not format — my first failure printed the raw `"{script}"` template. Worth one
+  line in the Gotchas: "in edition ≤2018 crates, always pass explicit format
+  arguments to assertion/panic messages."
+- **Gap: process-aborting failures during the 10x run are silent about their
+  input.** When the abort killed the test binary, hegel had no chance to report or
+  shrink, and the failure database recorded nothing; I had to add a temporary
+  `eprintln!` of drawn values and re-run with `--nocapture` in a loop. The
+  SKILL.md "Run and Reflect" section could mention this technique (stream draws
+  with `--nocapture` to identify abort-causing inputs), since the existing
+  abort-special-case text only covers what to do *after* the input is known.
+- **`sampled_from` requiring a `Vec` per draw** makes drawing from a `&'static
+  [&str]` table in a loop slightly awkward (`TOKENS.to_vec()` per draw). A
+  slice-accepting form would be nicer; not a blocker.
+- Everything else (imperative draws in helpers taking `&TestCase`, the recursive
+  `compose!`-boxed generator pattern for expression trees, `one_of!` boundary
+  boosting, `from_regex`) worked exactly as documented on the first try.
